/-- solkey `SolcControlFlow.sequentialAssignmentDoesNotSwap` (`solc/SolcControlFlow.sol`).

solc: various/swap_in_storage_overwrite.sol — `(x, y) = (y, x)` does not swap. solc
assigns the tuple components with no temporary, so the pair runs as `y = x; x = y;`
and both structs end up holding x's original value. Tuple assignment does not parse
here, so the port is that desugaring. -/
theorem sequentialAssignmentDoesNotSwap :
    Diamond (State.init SolcControlFlow)
    sol[SolcControlFlow]{
      sx.a = 1;
      sx.b = 2;
      sy.a = 3;
      sy.b = 4;
      sy = sx;
      sx = sy;
      assert(sx.a == 1);
      assert(sx.b == 2);
      assert(sy.a == 1);
      assert(sy.b == 2);
    } := by
  corpus_decide

/-- solkey `SolcControlFlow.swapViaTemporary` (`solc/SolcControlFlow.sol`).

solc: various/swap_in_storage_overwrite.sol — the corrected version, with the temporary
the upstream test shows is necessary. -/
theorem swapViaTemporary :
    Diamond (State.init SolcControlFlow)
    sol[SolcControlFlow]{
      sx.a = 1;
      sx.b = 2;
      sy.a = 3;
      sy.b = 4;
      Pair memory tmp = sx;
      sx = sy;
      sy = tmp;
      assert(sx.a == 3);
      assert(sx.b == 4);
      assert(sy.a == 1);
      assert(sy.b == 2);
    } := by
  corpus_decide

/-- solkey `SolcControlFlow.ternarySelectsFirstMemorySource` (`solc/SolcControlFlow.sol`).

solc: expressions/conditional_expression_storage_memory_1.sol — `data1 = cond ? x : y;`
with two memory sources, `cond` true.

concretized: solkey proves this for all parameters; ported at cond = 1.

solkey tags this `@custom:key box`; the port makes its assumptions true
instead, so the diamond also proves that it does not revert. -/
theorem ternarySelectsFirstMemorySource :
    Diamond (State.init SolcControlFlow)
    sol[SolcControlFlow]{
      uint cond = 1;
      bool c = cond == 1;
      Pair memory mx;
      mx.a = 11;
      Pair memory my;
      my.a = 22;
      target = c ? mx : my;
      assert(target.a == 11);
    } := by
  corpus_decide

